1. Create a JavaScript object to store inventory data:
```javascript
const inventory = {
  'item1': {
    quantity: 10,
    threshold: 5
  },
  'item2': {
    quantity: 20,
    threshold: 10
  }
};
```
1. Use the `setInterval` function to check inventory levels every minute:
```javascript
setInterval(function() {
  for (const item in inventory) {
    if (inventory[item].quantity <= inventory[item].threshold) {
      // Notify the inventory manager
      console.log(`${item} is running low!`);
    }
  }
}, 60000);
```
1. To ensure that the notifications for back-in-stock items are personalized for each customer's preferences, you can use JavaScript to store customer preferences and use them to filter inventory updates. Here's an example of how you could do this:
```javascript
const customers = [
  {
    name: 'John',
    preferences: ['item1', 'item3']
  },
  {
    name: 'Jane',
    preferences: ['item2', 'item4']
  }
];

function updateInventory(newInventory) {
  for (const customer of customers) {
    const filteredInventory = newInventory.filter(item => customer.preferences.includes(item));
    console.log(`Updates for ${customer.name}:`, filteredInventory);
  }
}
```
1. You can then use the `updateInventory` function to send personalized inventory updates to each customer when new inventory arrives.

As for HTML strategies, you can use the `<select>` element to allow customers to choose their preferred items, and use JavaScript to save these preferences in local storage or a server-side database. You can then use the `<div>` element to display personalized inventory updates for each customer.

