Here's a simple SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder for an FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out
);

reg [7:0] data_reg;
reg [3:0] parity_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_reg <= 8'b00000000;
        parity_reg <= 4'b0000;
    end else
    begin
        data_reg <= data_in;
        parity_reg <= parity_calc(data_reg);
    end
end

function [4:0] parity_calc(data);
    // Implementation of parity check calculation goes here
    // Return the parity bits as a 4-bit vector
endfunction

assign data_out = data_reg | parity_reg;

endmodule
```

This module has a clock input (clk), a reset input (reset), and two data inputs (data_in and data_out). The data_in signal is used to feed the data to be encoded, while the data_out signal is used to output the encoded data along with the parity bits.

The internal signals include a 8-bit data register (data_reg) and a 4-bit parity register (parity_reg). The data_reg is updated with the incoming data on each clock cycle, while the parity_reg is updated with the result of the parity calculation.

The parity calculation function (parity_calc) takes an 8-bit data vector as input and returns a 4-bit parity vector as output. You can implement the parity calculation logic according to your desired LDPC encoding scheme.

Finally, the data_out signal is assigned the value of the data_reg concatenated with the parity_reg. This ensures that the encoded data is outputted along with the parity bits.

