To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` object and the `User` property. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace WebApiExample.Controllers
{
    [Authorize]
    [Route("api/[controller]")]
    [ApiController]
    public class ValuesController : ControllerBase
    {
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult<string> Get()
        {
            var user = (ClaimsPrincipal)User;
            var username = user.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.Name).Value;

            return $"Hello, {username}!";
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we first check if the user is authorized to access the API using the `[Authorize]` attribute. If the user is authorized, we get the `ClaimsPrincipal` object from the `User` property. Then, we find the claim with the `ClaimTypes.Name` type and extract the value, which represents the username. Finally, we return a greeting message with the username.

Inner functions and outer functions are types of functions in programming languages. Inner functions are functions defined within another function, while outer functions are functions defined at the global level or outside of any other function.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```javascript
// Outer function
function greet(name) {
  console.log("Hello, " + name);
}

// Inner function
function greetInner() {
  console.log("Greetings from inside!");
}

// Calling the outer function
greet("John"); // Output: Hello, John

// Calling the inner function
greetInner(); // Output: Greetings from inside!
```

In this example, `greet` is an outer function that takes a name parameter and prints a greeting message. `greetInner` is an inner function that simply prints a message without any parameters. Both functions can be called independently, but the inner function is defined within the outer function.

