Here's a simple example of a toast notification using React Context:

```javascript
import React, { createContext, useState } from 'react';

const ToastContext = createContext();

function ToastProvider({ children }) {
  const [toast, setToast] = useState(null);

  function showToast(message) {
    setToast(message);
    setTimeout(() => {
      setToast(null);
    }, 3000);
  }

  return (
    <ToastContext.Provider value={{ showToast }}>
      {children}
    </ToastContext.Provider>
  );
}

function ToastConsumer() {
  const { showToast } = useContext(ToastContext);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => showToast('Hello, world!')}>Show Toast</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export { ToastProvider, ToastConsumer };
```

To use this component, you can wrap your application in the `ToastProvider` and use the `ToastConsumer` wherever you want to display the toast.

Analyzing a painting using C++ is an interesting approach, but it's important to note that C++ is primarily used for programming and not for analyzing visual art. However, you can use C++ to create tools or scripts that assist in the analysis of paintings based on various criteria such as form, content, context, etc.

Here's an example of a simple C++ program that reads in image data and performs some basic analysis:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <opencv2/core.hpp>
#include <opencv2/imgcodecs.hpp>
#include <opencv2/imgproc.hpp>

int main() {
    cv::Mat image;
    cv::imread("painting.jpg", image);

    // Convert to grayscale
    cv::Mat gray;
    cv::cvtColor(image, gray, cv::COLOR_BGR2GRAY);

    // Apply Gaussian blur
    cv::Mat blurred;
    cv::GaussianBlur(gray, blurred, cv::Size(5, 5), 0);

    // Detect edges
    cv::Mat edges;
    cv::Canny(blurred, edges, 50, 150);

    // Show the results
    cv::imshow("Gray", gray);
    cv::imshow("Blurred", blurred);
    cv::imshow("Edges", edges);

    return 0;
}
```

This program reads in an image (in this case, "painting.jpg"), converts it to grayscale, applies Gaussian blur, detects edges, and displays the results. You can further modify this code to perform more advanced analysis, such as color analysis, shape detection, and pattern recognition.

To incorporate art terminology and evaluate the painting based on various criteria, you can create a separate function or class that takes in the image data and returns a score or evaluation based on predefined rules or algorithms. For example:

```cpp
class PaintingEvaluator {
public:
