To inject JavaScript into an existing webpage using your Chrome extension, you'll need to create a background script and use content scripts. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a new folder for your extension and inside it, create the following files:
   - `manifest.json`
   - `background.js`
   - `content.js`

2. Open `manifest.json` and add the following content:

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Inject Script",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Inserts a script into a webpage",
  "browser_action": {
    "default_popup": "content.html",
    "default_icon": "icon.png"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab"]
}
```

3. Open `background.js` and add the following content:

```javascript
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(
  function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
    if (request.action === 'inject') {
      chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, tabs => {
        chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: request.script});
      });
    }
  }
);
```

4. Open `content.html` and add the following content:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Inject Script</title>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Inject Script</h1>
  <button id="inject">Inject Script</button>
  <script src="content.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

5. Open `content.js` and add the following content:

```javascript
document.getElementById('inject').addEventListener('click', () => {
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({action: 'inject', script: 'your_script.js'});
});
```

