It seems like you're generating the planets randomly within a certain range. To prevent them from being too close together, you can implement a simple collision detection system. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const otherPlanet = planets[i];
      const distance = planet.position.reduce((a, b) => a + b ** 2, 0);

      if (distance <= minDistance ** 2) {
        continue;
      }

      planets.push(planet);
      break;
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

This function will check if the new planet is too close to any existing planet before adding it to the list. If it is too close, it will skip it and try again. This should help prevent the planets from being too close together.

